pass, and then I still have one card left.\n\nAlternatively, after I play ♣2 and take control, I could consider **leading a low card** — but I don’t have any low cards. My only remaining cards are two Aces.\n\nSo if I play ♣2 now, I win the trick, then I must lead an Ace. Landlord passes (likely), teammate passes (if they can\'t beat it), then I play another Ace — landlord may pass again, teammate passes — then I\'m out. But this lets me finish first — which is acceptable, since both peasants win if either empties hand before landlord.\n\nBut better: if I **pass**, and teammate has a card that can be played later under a different lead, they might get a better chance. But right now, if I pass, landlord keeps control — bad.\n\nBest strategy: **I should take control now, because I have the only realistic chance to disrupt the landlord and potentially finish quickly**.\n\nBut wait — the landlord played ♣A. I can beat it with ♣2. If I do, I win the trick, and then I lead. I will lead one of my Aces — say ♠A. Then:\n- Landlord can pass (they have 5 cards)\n- Teammate has 1 card — if it\'s a 2 or joker, they *could* beat it, but they won\'t want to waste it unless necessary. More likely, they pass.\n- Then I play my last Ace.\n- Landlord may pass again.\n- Then I’m out.\n\nBut if my teammate has a low card, they never get to play it — unless someone leads a low card.\n\nAlternatively, if I **pass**, landlord wins with ♣A, then they lead next — they could lead a small card, say a 3 or 8, and then my teammate might be able to play their last card (if it matches), and go out — which would win the game.\n\nThat’s the key: **if I pass, the landlord leads next, and might give my teammate a chance to play their last card**.\n\nBut if I play ♣2, I take control, but then I lead an Ace — which my teammate likely cannot beat or match, so they pass, and I continue — but I end up finishing, not them.\n\nHowever, **either peasant winning is a win** — so me going out is fine.\n\nBut: if